    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3868": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives Job Code 3868 Department Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives provides strategic leadership for the identification, development, and advancement of new revenue-generating opportunities that support OCAD University’s long-term financial sustainability, institutional priorities, brand and community values. Reporting to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration (VPFA), the Executive Director is responsible for developing and implementing a comprehensive revenue diversification strategy focused on expanding existing revenue streams, identifying emerging business opportunities, and establishing new commercial ventures, partnerships and enterprise initiatives. The role works collaboratively across the University, including, but not limited to: the School of Continuing Studies, Copy & Print Services, Merchandise & Design Product Sales and Event Sales. The role operates at a strategic level, assessing market opportunities, developing business cases driving business performance, and identifying new opportunities for revenue growth. The Executive Director serves as a central catalyst for revenue growth and business development across the institution. While the role does not assume day-to-day operational responsibility for existing units or override the authority of leaders currently accountable for the various portfolios, the Executive Director is expected to work alongside established unit leaders to enhance revenue generation, operational sustainability, market reach and institutional growth. Success in this role requires the ability to influence, align, and mobilize cross-functional teams within this shared accountability framework to advance integrated revenue growth strategies. This is one (1) year appointment with the possibility of renewal dependent on the metrics of success and growth. Within six (6) months of the term, the incumbent will be expected to develop and present a strategic plan for the University that allows for revenue growth within the associated departments listed above. Summary of Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership & Revenue Growth Develop and implement a comprehensive auxiliary revenue diversification strategy aligned with OCAD U’s strategic, academic, financial and operational priorities. Lead the development  of formal business plans, feasibility studies, financial models and implementation plans for new revenue initiatives. Identify and evaluate new revenue-generating opportunities, partnerships, and commercial ventures, including market expansion and innovation initiatives. Ensure auxiliary operations contribute meaningfully to institutional financial sustainability targets. Enterprise Growth Initiatives In collaboration with members of the Executive Team, lead cross-functional initiatives designed to generate new sources of institutional revenue Establish and monitor key performance indicators (KPIs), financial targets, and benchmarking measures aligned with sector best practices. Collaborate with leaders to identify new opportunities for professional learning, auxiliary services, facility use, and partnership development. Facilitate the exploration and development of new enterprises and revenue-generating services. Drive continuous improvement, innovation, and cost optimization across auxiliary functions. Support existing unit leaders in assessing opportunities for revenue expansion, while respecting their operational accountability and decision-making authority within their portfolios. Develop frameworks to help prioritize revenue opportunities based on strategic alignment, financial viability, market demand, operational capacity, risk and institutional impact. Financial Management & Business Planning Develop financial projections, business models, revenue forecasts, and return on investment analyses for proposed revenue initiatives. Ensure rigorous financial analysis, performance tracking, and reporting frameworks are in place to support decision-making. Monitor and evaluate the performance of approved revenue diversification initiatives against established targets, timelines and key performance indicators. Advise pricing strategies, contract negotiations, and commercial agreements to maximize revenue and mitigate risk in collaboration with unit heads to ensure new business initiatives align with the University’s long-term strategy Support integrated planning across revenue-generating activities to reduce duplication, identify synergies and align initiatives with institutional priorities. Ensure financial analysis and reporting frameworks support transparent, evidence-informed decision-making Governance, Reporting & Accountability Ensure strong governance, compliance, and risk management practices across auxiliary enterprises. Develops implementation plans for approved revenue initiatives and monitors execution progress, reporting regularly to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration on status, outcomes, risks, and corrective actions required Provide no less frequent than semi-annual updates to the Audit, Finance and Risk Committee (AFRC) on operations, revenue metrics, and KPI achievement. Prepare and present strategic reports and recommendations to senior leadership and governing bodies. Ensure transparency and alignment with institutional policies, regulatory requirements, and public-sector accountability expectations. Stakeholder Engagement & Partnerships Build and maintain strong relationships with internal stakeholders, including Deans, administrative leaders, and service units. Develop and manage external partnerships with vendors, service providers, and commercial collaborators. Represent the University in negotiations with external partners and business development initiatives. People Leadership Lead project teams, working groups, and cross-functional initiatives in support of revenue diversification objectives. Foster a culture of accountability, innovation, collaboration, and service excellence. Provide strategic guidance, business development expertise, and mentorship to colleagues engaged in revenue-generating initiatives. Support change management activities associated with the development and implementation of new revenue opportunities. Qualifications: A graduate degree in Business Administration, Commerce, Finance, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience with special consideration for the professional and community-based experience of equity-deserving groups Minimum ten (10) years of progressive leadership experience in revenue-generating operations, auxiliary services, commercial enterprises, or related fields. Experience within a post-secondary institution or similarly complex, public-sector environment is strongly preferred, with specific experience and knowledge of Continuing Studies in higher education. Demonstrated success in developing business strategies, growing revenue, and managing multi-unit operations. Experience reporting to senior executives and governance bodies (e.g., Board committees). Strong business acumen, financial management, and strategic planning capabilities. Demonstrated ability to drive revenue growth while balancing mission and public accountability. Expertise in business case development, financial modeling, and scenario planning. Exceptional stakeholder management and influencing skills. Strong communication skills, including executive-level presentation and reporting. Ability to lead change, innovation, and complex organizational initiatives. Ability to both think and act strategically, balancing vision with the ability to implement that vision. A collaborative and consultative working style with an ability to develop and maintain effective working relationships internally and externally in a manner that gains & maintains trust, respect and cooperation. Excellent interpersonal skills and judgement, with a demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive or confidential matters with discretion. Personal qualities that include integrity, openness, fairness, resourcefulness, and confidence, coupled with an accessible style, optimism, enthusiasm and high emotional intelligence Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, decolonization and Indigenization. Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $138,200.00 to $162,554.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin September 21 st 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3877": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Graduate Research Assistant: Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure Job Code 3877 Department Research Labs & Projects Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now Graduate Research Assistant: Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. Project Details: Can a power grid be an artistic medium? What about a water pipe? Or a railway? Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure asks what happens when artists treat infrastructure itself as raw material, using it to expose how systems distribute resources unevenly and to imagine how those systems can be assembled differently. Earlier case studies explored creative responses to South Africa’s electricity and water crises; the next stage of the project will turn to public transportation, sanitation, and cellular networks across Southern Africa. The Research Assistant will work closely with Principal Investigator (PI), Dr. Anna Stielau in Fall 2026 to develop the project’s next phase and prepare a SSHRC Insight Development Grant application. Applications are particularly encouraged from MA, MFA, MDes, and other graduate students whose work intersects with contemporary art, media and technology, African studies, infrastructure and the built environment, or postcolonial and decolonial studies. Expertise in African art or history is welcome but not required. The position will provide hands-on experience in developing a major humanities research project from its earliest stages. The successful candidate will gain experience in interdisciplinary research, building literature reviews, evaluating case studies, and the practical mechanics of preparing a competitive grant application in Canada. Responsibilities Working under the supervision of the Principal Investigator, Dr. Stielau, in support of the project's research goals and objectives Researching contemporary Southern African artists, artworks, exhibitions, and institutional collections to identify potential case studies Conducting interdisciplinary literature searches to assist in developing an annotated project bibliography Locating, organizing, and synthesizing relevant scholarship and archival material, and preparing concise research summaries for discussion with the PI Assisting with research related to the project’s SSHRC Insight Development Grant application, including fact-finding around the proposed fieldwork budget Assisting with reference checking, document preparation, and final assembly of grant materials in accordance with SSHRC requirements Participating in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities Qualifications Current enrollment in a relevant graduate program, with priority given to OCAD University students Strong research, reading, and written communication skills Strong organizational skills and attention to detail Ability to work independently and communicate clearly and consistently about research progress Interest in one or more of the following areas: contemporary art and visual culture; media and technology; infrastructure and the built environment; African or Global South art and media; postcolonial/decolonial studies. Previous experience working with scholarly databases, conducting in-depth research, and using Zotero or other citation-management software is an asset Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Hours of Work: Approximately 100 hours total over the period of September 2026 to February 2027. Scheduling will be arranged in consultation with the successful candidate. Work Environment: The position will primarily involve remote research, with occasional in-person or Teams meetings with the Principal Investigator at 100 McCaul St, as required. Compensation: $37.28 per hour, plus 6% in lieu of benefits plus vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act Please note the following Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. Current students at OCAD University will be given priority consideration for all Research Assistant positions, with the highest priority given to those not currently holding any other TA/RA positions at the University. Applications should include: A one-page cover letter explaining the candidate’s interest A curriculum vitae The contact information for three (3) referees Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence addressed to Dr. Anna Stielau, Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture, Faculty of Arts and Science , online by clicking the “Apply for this Position” button below. Shortlisted candidates may be invited to a brief conversation with the PI to discuss their interests and experience. The review of applications will begin on September 20, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3836": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Indigenous Student Success Coordinator Job Code 3836 Department Indigenous Student Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre (ISC), the Indigenous Student Success Coordinator is responsible for providing Indigenous students with academic support, referrals to student support services both on and off campus, as well as enhancing Indigenous students’ experience through academic and cultural supports, programming, and activities. The Coordinator is responsive to individual student needs, providing support with financial aid, academic advising, and offering advocacy and guidance with academic and non-academic policies. The Coordinator is responsible for planning and implementing services and programs designed to facilitate the academic success of Indigenous students at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Coordinator works to ensure that Indigenous students are supported with culturally relevant services, working closely with Indigenous Student Centre staff, faculty and OCAD U support services to facilitate open, respectful communication to meet the needs of Indigenous students.  Regular on-site attendance is an essential requirement of this position to ensure accessible, responsive, and culturally appropriate support for students. Summary of Responsibilities: Responsible for coordinating, supporting, and enhancing core Indigenous Student Centre weekly programming and workshops aimed at supporting Indigenous students’ social, cultural and emotional wellbeing Collaborate with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre and ISC team to create new programs that enhance the Indigenous student experience at OCAD University; support and facilitate the development of student-led programming in partnership with Indigenous students and student monitors. Maintain a consistent on-campus presence to provide support and referrals to Indigenous students in distress and as needed; act as a liaison between students and available supports Provide advocacy services and guidance to Indigenous students navigating academic and non-academic policies and processes such as academic misconduct, appeals, and petitions; formally advocate on behalf of Indigenous students and provide support through the relevant policy or process as needed or requested Provide academic advising, support and information to students pertaining to procedures, program requirements, dates and deadlines; work closely with Student Advising, Office of the Registrar and relevant support units Maintain current knowledge and comprehensive understanding of relevant academic and non-academic policies and processes including Student Petitions, Grade Appeals, Non-Academic Misconduct Policy, Response to Violent or Threatening Behaviour, Respectful Work and Learning Environment Policy; to effectively support student concerns and referrals to the relevant policy holder when necessary Maintain proactive communications, including the ISC weekly newsletter, social media accounts and the department’s website, to build awareness, promote events and ensure access to accurate, timely and relevant information; to connect with Indigenous OCAD U students, staff, faculty, alumni, Indigenous communities and partners Provide information and guidance to Indigenous students regarding internal and external bursaries, awards, financial supports; work closely with the Financial Aid & Awards office, providing referrals as needed Maintain knowledge of housing supports available for Indigenous OCAD U students; community programs, resources and housing partners Coordinate Orientation and Welcome events for new students and represent the Indigenous Student Centre at University events. With support from the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, maintain collaborative relationships with OCAD U’s existing student support services to ensure Indigenous student needs are integrated into service delivery throughout the University; including Student Advising, Office of the Registrar, Student Wellness Centre, Student Accessibility Services, Centre for Learning and Teaching, Financial Aid & Awards, Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers Maintain a resource list for external agencies and organizations that provide supports for Indigenous students, including grants and awards, housing, career opportunities, cultural supports, and health and wellness Track persistence and retention of Indigenous students on an ongoing basis and develop retention plans in collaboration with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre Supervise the Centre’s Student Monitors including participating in hiring, managing schedules, performance and approving biweekly time entry Serve on internal and external committees as needed Support the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre in other related functions and special projects contributing successful operation of the Indigenous Student Centre Qualifications: Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Métis, Inuit), connection to an Indigenous community, lived experience and an understanding of the Indigenous culture(s) and practices Experience with the administration of Indigenous community programming, education, and arts Understanding of Indigenous cultural, educational, political, and economic conditions Post-secondary degree or equivalent, with three (3) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service in a diverse environment Excellent written and oral communication skills, interpersonal skills, and strong self-management skills Demonstrated professionalism, and the ability to exercise tact, good judgment and diplomacy Strong working knowledge of all Microsoft Office applications, and experience working with databases Demonstrated attention to detail, strong organizational and time management skills, and the ability to juggle competing demands, prioritize tasks and meet deadlines Demonstrated ability to take initiative and be an active contributor in the planning, formulation and implementation of student-centered services Proven ability to work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Flexibility in work hours; must be willing and able to work occasional evenings, as required Mode of Work: O n-campus 5 days per week Compensation: Hiring Range - $31.59 to $36.14 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $31.59 to $41.52 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until May 31, 2027. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin August 7th, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3864": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Lead, Alumni Engagement Job Code 3864 Department Advancement Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Director of Annual Giving & Alumni Relations and working closely with academic faculties and programs, Marketing & Communications, the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers, Admissions & Recruitment, Advancement, and other campus partners, the Lead, Alumni Engagement will assess, structure, and expand ways for alumni to be involved, creating a comprehensive roster of engagement opportunities that leverage alumni expertise and strengthen connections between alumni, students, faculty, and the broader OCAD U community. This role will identify and assess existing alumni engagement activities across the institution, establish processes that support coordination and collaboration, and develop new initiatives to address gaps and broaden opportunities for meaningful alumni participation. The position will contribute to building a more connected and engaged alumni community while supporting institutional priorities related to student success, experiential learning, mentorship, recruitment, and lifelong engagement. OCAD University is seeking a Lead, Alumni Engagement to help strengthen and coordinate alumni engagement across the institution. This one-year term position will play a critical role in creating a more centralized and strategic approach to alumni engagement. Summary of Responsibilities: Serve as the University's central point of contact for alumni engagement activities, providing coordination, guidance, and support across faculties, departments, and administrative units. Conduct an inventory of existing alumni engagement initiatives and develop and maintain a centralized roster of opportunities for alumni involvement. Create and implement an annual alumni engagement plan that supports institutional priorities and advances a coordinated, university-wide approach to alumni relations. Collaborate with academic faculties and departments to support alumni engagement objectives and strengthen connections between alumni and academic programs. Partner with the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers to expand opportunities for alumni involvement in mentorship, experiential learning, career development, and other student-facing initiatives. Work with Recruitment & Admissions to develop meaningful opportunities for alumni participation in student recruitment activities, including ambassador programs, portfolio reviews, outreach initiatives, and prospective student engagement. Identify gaps in existing programming and develop new initiatives that enhance alumni participation and strengthen connections among alumni, students, faculty, and OCAD U. Collaborate with Advancement and Marketing & Communications to promote alumni programs and opportunities through a variety of communication channels. Establish processes for tracking alumni engagement, maintain accurate records in Raiser's Edge and other systems, and monitor participation and outcomes. Engage with alumni to understand their interests, identify opportunities for involvement, and assess their willingness to participate in alumni programs and initiatives. Represent the Alumni Relations team at events by welcoming attendees and fostering meaningful connections with alumni. Evaluate the effectiveness of alumni engagement activities and provide recommendations for continuous improvement and long-term sustainability. Qualifications: Minimum of five (5) years of experience in alumni relations, community engagement, or stakeholder relations Post secondary degree, or equivalent Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, including alumni, students, faculty, staff, volunteers, and external partners. Experience developing, coordinating, and implementing engagement programs, events, or volunteer initiatives. Strong project management and organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines in a collaborative environment. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to tailor messaging to diverse audiences. Strong analytical skills and experience collecting, tracking, and interpreting participation and engagement data to inform decision-making and evaluate outcomes. Experience working with constituent relationship management (CRM) systems; familiarity with Raiser's Edge or similar advancement databases is considered an asset. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively across departments and organizational levels. Knowledge of higher education, alumni relations, advancement, student engagement, or the arts and design sector is an asset. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $72,077.00 annually to $84,784.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Tuesday August 25 th and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3782": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description PhD or PostDoc Position in Equitable and Accessible AI Job Code 3782 Department Inclusive Design Research Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create The Inclusive Design Research Centre seeks a researcher/developer at the post-doctoral or PhD candidate level to join an interdisciplinary team co-designing and developing tools and policies that address the opportunities and risks of AI for people and communities experiencing accessibility and/or intersectional barriers. We take an inclusive co-design approach, exploring the technical, legal, policy, social and person-specific dimensions to co-design more equitable and accessible AI. About the Role We are looking for a researcher/developer to collaborate in exploring how AI technologies can be more accessible and equitably designed, deployed, used, governed, and challenged. This could involve analysing and measuring how systems operate in practice, conducting research into their use and impact, co-designing and developing tools, technical mechanisms, and governance/regulatory strategies that support effective accessibility, equity, accountability, transparency, and oversight. You will join a supportive interdisciplinary co-design team to address critical challenges in understanding and shaping how AI systems are approached, developed, used, and governed to align with inclusive design values. Who We Are Looking For We are seeking candidates with technical knowledge and skills in AI development to complement a team of disability community members, inclusive designers, inclusive developers, researchers and global AI expert advisors. Responsibilities Participate in a co-design team to offer technical expertise and develop prototypes that respond to criteria specified by intended beneficiaries Iteratively refine approaches in response to feedback Support the supervision, instruction, mentoring, and development of students/junior researchers. Collaborate with disability communities and researchers, contributing to a supportive and engaged co-design environment. Contribute to shaping new research agendas, funding proposals, and collaborative projects within and beyond the group. Help drive broader impact activities, including engagement with policy, industry, or the public where appropriate. Qualifications: A PhD degree (completed, in progress, near-completion, or equivalent experience) in a relevant discipline A demonstrated interest in accessible and equitable AI A demonstrated ability in applying current AI development tools to address AI risks and opportunities for minorities The ability to work independently and collaboratively in a diverse team setting Candidate has experience with web-app programming -- HTML, CSS, JavaScript and TypeScript. Experience with the following is considered an asset: ARIA, Working with git and github Preact Vite and Jest. Natural language processing (NLP) experience, e.g, spaCy, whisper Willing to read materials about Blisssymbolics and acquire a baseline understanding of the Bliss language and how it works. Experience with AAC devices and access methods. Knowledge of Unicode and working with fonts. Opportunities and Environment Candidates will have access to: A highly interdisciplinary and collaborative research environment. Opportunities to engage with community organizations, as well as public and private organizations driving policy discussions Opportunities for engagement in conferences, workshops, and research collaborations. A global network of individuals and organizations working on accessible and equitable AI. What we offer This position is embedded in a creative, dynamic, and internationally renowned inclusive design team. Your research will play a crucial role in addressing the challenges AI poses for historically marginalized communities. You will be introduced to a multi-sector international community committed to more equitable systems. We prioritize a balanced and family-friendly work-life relationship, offering options for flexible working hours and part-time remote home-office arrangement Hours of Work: Full-time, 6-month contract; 35 hours per week. Work Environment: Remote, a shared office environment is available if preferred Compensation: $55,000 per year, inclusive of 4% vacation pay, plus 6% in lieu of benefits Application Process: Please submit the following as one PDF document: A one-page cover letter explaining your interest in this specific role and how your technical skills and research interests align with the project's focus on ethical AI and diverse knowledge systems. Your Curriculum Vitae (CV). The contact information for three (3) professional referees. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated *CV and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will be ongoing and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
