    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3876": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Program Development Specialist Job Code 3876 Department Admissions & Recruitment Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Business Strategy, the Program Development Specialist develops non-credit learning opportunities that highlight the value and increase the profile of OCAD University’s (OCAD U) community of artists and designers. The incumbent supports the development of market-driven, innovative non-credit learning opportunities in art & design that meet the aspirations of learners and emerging creatives, while enhancing OCAD U’s local, national and global impact. This role emphasizes the engagement of alumni, industry and community partners in program development and special projects and works within a business development context to ensure programs are sustainable and return value to OCAD U. Summary of Responsibilities : Program Development, Research, and Admin Collaborate closely with faculty, industry professionals, and stakeholders to develop specialized non-credit programs and courses tailored to the needs and aspirations of mid-career, emerging professionals in art and design and other potential learner segments that hold strategic value to OCAD U Work closely with international partners to develop and deliver short courses, workshops and residencies Support the delivery of existing non-credit programs, including supporting incoming participants with registrations, drafting and distributing relevant communications, and general support, through the CRM and Registrations systems Work closely with the Manager to ensure programs are financially sustainable and are relevant to new and emerging markets Lead the development and submission of grant applications and other external funding opportunities, serving as the primary point of contact and consulting with the Manager as required Assess and advise on the viability and feasibility of new learning opportunities as consulted Prepare budget proposals, monitor expenditures, and ensure fiscal responsibility in accordance with university policies; provide administrative support in managing program budgets, financial resources, and procurement processes Recruit, onboard, and support a diverse team of instructors and industry experts who bring practical experience and specialized knowledge to the classroom; ensure programs integrate opportunities for alumni engagement Provide insights, data, and reports to inform strategic marketing, branding, and advertising opportunities Work closely with the Manager and Director, Learning Pathways, to research, identify and propose networks, partnerships and relationships that enhance the relevance of art & design education for lifelong learners and workforce development across various sectors Develop and advance new and existing learner-centred frameworks and design thinking principles to invite and develop emerging, market-relevant learning opportunities. Work with campus stakeholders (e.g., School of Continuing Studies, Admissions, Recruitment) to map program proposals against other offerings, policies, and procedures to ensure they are distinct, have a clear value proposition, and are viable Work closely with Marketing & Communications to create communication and marketing materials that are relevant to learners, promote enrolment in special programs, increase program visibility, and attract prospective learners, as needed Relationship Development Cultivate relationships with alumni to encourage their participation in non-credit program development, advisory activities, and guest lectures Collaborate with the Alumni Relations team to ensure alumni relationships are cultivated at an institutional level Support the development and implementation of the strategic development plan by coordinating and facilitating engagement activities with key partners through events, public engagements, workshops, and co-design sessions Student-Centred Advising and Support Provide support and guidance to learners for program selection, application, and registration as needed, including 1:1 advising, presentations and review of supplemental application material (ie. portfolios, written statements). Work with the Manager to determine the admissibility and readiness of learners to enrol in application-based programs. Contribute to the development of diversity, equity, inclusivity, and accessibility (DEIA) strategies throughout all programs, services and communications Support marketing and recruitment events, activities and initiatives as needed, which may include occasional weekend and evening hours. Support the Manager and Director in other related projects that contribute to the successful operation of the Admissions, Recruitment and Continuing Studies departments. Qualifications : Master’s degree in Art, Design, Education, or a related field Four (4) years of experience in program management, preferably in adult learning environments Demonstrated expertise in developing and delivering educational programs tailored to mid-career professionals Demonstrated research and analytical skills, including the ability to assess market trends, learner needs, and emerging opportunities Experience with the LMS; experience with the CRM and registration system is required Strong understanding of curriculum development, instructional design, and educational best practices Exceptional leadership, communication, and organizational skills Proven ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders and build strong partnerships Experience developing and sustaining strategic partnerships that support program development, learner engagement, and organizational objectives Commitment to fostering a supportive and inclusive learning environment Commitment to support research-driven projects in cross functional teams Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for student, staff, and faculty Mode of Work: On campus, 3 days per week. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of four days per week of on-campus work is required, with flexibility to adjust on-campus days and hours to meet department needs. Occasional evening and weekend work will be required, with shift premiums applied in accordance with the Collective Agreement. All flexibility and scheduling provisions are subject to the terms outlined in the Collective Agreement. Compensation: Hiring Range - $66,925.74 to $76,724.74 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $66,925.74 to $87,958.44 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This posting is for an existing position. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applicants will begin September 11, 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3858": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Sessional Instructor: Foresight Studio, School of Graduate Studies, Fall/Winter 2026-27 Job Code 3858 Department Graduate Studies Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The School of Graduate Studies invites applications for the following anticipated course offerings in the 2026-2027 academic year : SFIN-6021-301 Foresight Studio ( Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – December 8, 2026; Wednesday, 6:30 p.m. - 9:30 p.m., Synchronous Online) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This studio course will introduce foresight methods in the development of strategic proposals in the private, public, voluntary or ‘for-benefit’ sector. Working in teams, students will identify an issue in a specific sector and will begin their exploration and research in a divergent process of signal discovery through methods such as environmental scanning, new technology research, user research, field study, or stakeholder workshops. This phase of work frames the problem. In a convergent process, students will apply methodologies, which include medium- to long-range scenario planning and technology adoption modelling to develop creative insights and implications for action. Students with credit in SFIN-6C02 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Teaching and teaching-related responsibilities may include but are not limited to: course outline development, studio preparation and delivery inclusive of lectures, critiques, grading, mid-term evaluations and final evaluations/reviews; including responsibility for health & safety and meeting academic and curricular requirements and deadlines. QUALIFICATIONS: The successful applicant should have a terminal degree in art, design or relevant discipline and/or equivalent professional/practical experience Experience teaching at the post-secondary level is considered an asset, equivalent relevant experience will be considered Outstanding interpersonal and communication skills Demonstrated leadership and experience promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and faculty TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Sessional appointments are fixed-term annual contracts. Sessional compensation includes a per-course pay scale with salary level of $7,508.00 ( per course) plus 7% in lieu of benefits and vacation pay, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. These positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. Applications should include: 1. A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying relevant courses above; 2. A curriculum vitae; 3. A statement of teaching philosophy that responds to the profile of the position; 4. A link or web address to your digital portfolio of recent and current work/publications (if applicable) 5. And the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all five (5) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, addressed to School of Graduate Studies Appointments Committee, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below. The review of applications will begin on August 11, 2026 at 4:00pm and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized and Indigenous faculty, priority in hiring will be given to qualified racialized and Indigenous persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3866": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader): A History of the Métis, Faculty of Arts & Science, Fall 2026 Job Code 3866 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Arts & Science invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Fall 2026 semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and meeting target registration in courses. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. IVCV-2002-301: A History of the Métis (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Mondays: 8:30AM - 11:30AM; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: The Métis are a distinct Indigenous people with a unique history, culture, language and territory. This course will reflect on notions of Metis identity through the lens of Métis visual culture. From the vibrant material culture of early Métis makers to current Métis artists and filmmakers, the non-linear path of this course focuses on analyzing the work in relation to historic and contemporary issues around land, tradition, language, spirit, colonialism and decolonization. Students with credit in IVCL-3001, HUMN-3012, HUMN-3B16 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3869": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader): Astronomy-Scales & Structures, Faculty of Arts & Science, Winter 2025 Job Code 3869 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Arts & Science invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Fall 2026 semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and meeting target registration in courses. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. SCTM-2003-301: Astronomy-Scales & Structures (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Wednesday: 11:50AM - 2:50PM; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This course will explore the science of Astronomy from the perspective of scale and structure: how, from the microscopic scales of atoms, molecules and light, we have come to understand the macroscopic scales of stars, nebulae, galaxies, and the large scale structure of the Universe. Astronomy is an intensely visual science, with exquisite structures and morphologies revealed by increasingly sophisticated technologies, probing wavelengths the human eye can and cannot see; yet surprisingly the visual contains only a small fraction of the known Universe. Instead, “Dark Matter” and “Dark Energy” make up the bulk of our Universe. We will examine the scientific method of astronomers, and how they have reached such unanticipated results. Students with credit in SCTM-2B03 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3872": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader): Intro Comp Sci-Logic & Coding, Faculty of Arts & Science, Fall 2026 Job Code 3872 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. SCTM-2005-301: Intro Comp Sci-Logic & Coding (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Fridays: 8:30AM - 11:30AM; Online) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This course introduces students to the fundamentals of logic, and computer programming. It emphasizes object-oriented languages, allowing students to understand and later develop capacity in a wider range of programming methods. The material will be contextualized within both art and design practices, introducing students to programming for environments, objects and online, as well as practices of interaction, automation, generation, networks and visualization. Students will understand how to incorporate and recombine existing code and to use established design patterns. Basic foundational coding languages will be used to introduce key concepts that students can employ in their subsequent study of advanced programming languages . Students will be asked to bring in work-in-progress from their studio courses that can be implemented through coding. Students with credit in SCTM-2B06 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3871": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader): The Science of Colour, Faculty of Arts & Science, Fall 2026 Job Code 3871 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Arts & Science invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Fall 2026 semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and meeting target registration in courses. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. SCTM-2012-301: The Science of Colour (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Tuesday: 11:50AM - 2:50PM; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: A cross-disciplinary approach in examining colour, with the aim of understanding colour from the multiple viewpoints of art, physics, chemistry, physiology and history. Topics include: perception, wave nature of light, spectroscopy, colour harmony and contrast, natural phenomena, dyes and pigments. Students with credit in SCTM-2B22 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3867": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader) Science Fiction, Faculty of Arts & Science, Fall 2026 Job Code 3867 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Arts & Science invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Fall 2026 semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and meeting target registration in courses. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. ENGL-2017-301: Science Fiction (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Thursdays: 8:30AM - 11:30AM; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This course explores the science fiction genre as a lens for examining cultural, technological, and philosophical questions. Through novels, short stories, and films, students will analyze how science fiction reflects and critiques scientific advancements, societal change, and human identity. Topics include artificial intelligence, space exploration, dystopian futures, and posthumanism. The course also highlights emerging subgenres such as green science fiction and climate fiction (\"cli-fi\"), exploring how speculative narratives address environmental crises and imagine sustainable futures. Readings span classic and contemporary works, encouraging critical engagement with speculative worlds. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3862": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Tier II, Canada Research Chair: Artificial Intelligence and Creativity Job Code 3862 Department Research Office Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University (OCAD U) acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishnaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we gather and create. OCAD University, Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious, and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable, and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum, and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national, and global leader in art and design. Students benefit from hands-on studio learning and have access to state-of-the-art shops and studios for both traditional and digital creation. Alongside a diverse and supportive team of instructors and peers, students gain employable skills. The School of Graduate Studies offers numerous programs where students may earn a Masters of Fine Arts or Masters of Design: Strategic Foresight and Innovation, Inclusive Design, Design for Health, Interdisciplinary Art/Media/Design, Digital Futures, and Criticism and Curatorial Practice. The University offers BA, BFA, and BDes degrees in Advertising, Creative Writing, Criticism and Curatorial Practice, Digital Futures, Drawing and Painting, Environmental Design, Experimental Animation, Graphic Design, Illustration, Indigenous Visual Culture, Industrial Design, Integrated Media, Interior Design, Material Art and Design, Photography, Printmaking and Publications, Scultpure/Installation, and Visual and Critical Studies. OCAD U graduates work in a variety of sectors such as advertising, art history and curatorial studies, digital futures, strategic foresight, inclusive design, health design, urban planning, environmental design, gaming, film, animation, publishing, illustration, graphic design, product design and consultancy, material-based design, visual arts, and arts administration. TENURE-TRACK POSITION: ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR TIER II CANADA RESEARCH CHAIR in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity Posted August 7, 2026 OCAD University invites applications for a full-time Tenure-Track Tier 2 Canada Research Chair in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. The position is expected to commence January 1 or July 1, 2028, and is subject to budgetary approval. In addition, this position is contingent on the candidate’s successful application to the Canada Research Chair (Tier 2) program. The successful candidate will be required to participate in preparation of a CRC nomination package for submission in April 2027. The successful candidate will demonstrate intersectional expertise in the context of or related to AI & Creativity in areas such as: generative art and computational creativity studio-based AI research, artist-led machine learning, creative coding and software art, digital fabrication and AI-assisted making, interactive and immersive media, performance and time-based AI practice, human-AI collaboration in creative process, critical making and speculative design, sound art and AI-generated composition, film, animation and AI cinematics, interactive and immersive media, game design and procedural world-building, wearable and embodied AI systems, decolonial approaches to creative AI, AI and craft-based practice, community-engaged creative AI practice, algorithmic justice, ethical making. Bringing together studio practice and computational research, the Canada Research Chair (CRC) in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity will provide visionary leadership for the development, critique, and application of artificial intelligence within expanded creative and artistic fields — centring the studio as a site of critical inquiry, cultural production, and technological imagination. The Canada Research Chair Tier 2 is for exceptional researchers, acknowledged by their peers as having the potential to lead in their field. The successful candidate should have experience as a Principal Investigator on at least one major, multi-year Tri-agency grant (NSERC, SSHRC or CIHR) or other major national/international academic grant, either held within the last five years or currently active. SUMMARY OF RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will: Build research capacity and outputs in keeping with the OCAD University Strategic Research Plan and program criteria for a Tier 2 CRC ; Maintain an active research and practice profile with a focus on AI and Creativity; Demonstrate a broad understanding of the impact of AI on areas such as creative practice, business, health, climate change, social, and cultural contexts; Promote and augment interdisciplinary research as an individual and a leader of collaborations; Build and maintain internal and external networks of scholars, stakeholders and collaborators; Teach in one or more undergraduate and graduate programs within the most appropriate home faculty or across faculties; Assume a key role in curriculum development related to the AI and Creativity; Provide service to the Faculty and University-wide governance system, as well as activities and outreach initiatives that contribute to the quality of academic and student life and University culture; Develop a plan to attract major national and international grants to support their research program; Engage in development of policies and training at the University. QUALIFICATIONS The ideal candidate will have: A PhD in a field relevant to AI and Creativity; exceptional creative scholars/practitioners with a Masters degree will also be considered as a doctoral degree is not required by the Canada Research Chairs Program. A minimum of 5 years relevant teaching experience at the post-secondary level (or equivalent experience); Outstanding and innovative research skills and accomplishments which will lead them to a strong international reputation as a leader, innovator, and cultural practitioner with demonstrated commitment to AI and Creativity; An original and innovative cross-disciplinary research program of the highest quality. Experience and conceptual understanding of intersectional AI and Creativity practices; A significant research/exhibition and publication record consistent with a Tier II CRC including a proven successful track record with scholarly and/or arts granting councils Familiarity and proven funding success. Canadian applicants must show success with one or more of the Tri-agencies (SSHRC, NSERC or CIHR) demonstrated by holding principal investigator status on past or current Tri-agency grants; International candidates must show success with one or more major research grants at a federal or senior external level. Participating as co-applicant and/or collaborator on tri-agency or other national grants will be an asset. Experience supervising graduate students at the Masters level (PhD supervision experience is an asset) Capacity to attract excellent graduate students and future researchers as well as play a leadership role in developing new talent and increasing the research capacity of OCAD University; Experience in curriculum development and working to develop and enhance undergraduate and graduate programs A demonstrated commitment to the principles and lived experiences of equity and diversity, and proven ability to work effectively and collegially with a diverse population; A proven record of research and/or professional practice relevant to qualifications, experience and discipline; Experience proactively advancing equity and sustainability in curriculum, teaching, scholarship, and creative/professional activities; Demonstrated understanding of the ways in which equity, Indigenous knowledge, and sustainability are fundamental to the quality of student experience, to innovative scholarship, and to creative production; and A proven record of promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and faculty. Tier 2 Chairs are intended for exceptional emerging scholars. Applicants who are more than 10 years from their first independent academic research-related appointment may have their eligibility for a Tier 2 Chair assessed through the program’s Tier 2 justification process . Please contact research@ocadu.ca for more information. OCAD University recognizes that life circumstances such as illness, disability, family and community responsibilities (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leaves due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, or slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability) may have an impact on a candidate’s record of research achievement.  These impacts will be taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. REMUNERATION Salary and rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications with access to full benefits, and pension contribution after one year of service. Canada Research Chairs are subject to review and approval by the Tri-agency Institutional Programs Secretariat. APPLICATION REQUIREMENTS & DEADLINE Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of research, teaching, professional practice, and service to Faculty and University-wide governance; A curriculum vitae; A statement demonstrating the candidate’s research philosophy and equity/diversity work; Evidence of teaching and research effectiveness (such as sample assignments, student feedback, course evaluations, records of successful research grants, etc.); A link to a digital portfolio of recent work/publications; The names and contact information of three referees. Please upload all six (6) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their electronic application through the portal in confidence addressed to: Ashok Mathur, Ph.D., Vice-President, Research & Dean, School of Graduate Studies OCAD University The review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to, and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of equity-seeking groups to self-identify within their letter of intent in their application . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized individuals and Indigenous persons in the ranks of tenure-track faculty and academic administrators, priority in hiring will be given to qualified persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture or Alli O’Shea , People & Culture Advisor, for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3880": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Black Student Community Animator Job Code 3880 Department Student Affairs Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Director, Office of Student Affairs, with day-to-day supervision from the Lead, Campus Life, the Black Student Community Animator supports the development and delivery of initiatives that foster connection, belonging, leadership development, and community-building among Black-identifying students at OCAD University. Working collaboratively with student leaders, campus partners, faculty and staff, the role assists with the planning, promotion and coordination of programs, events and engagement opportunities that support Black student flourishing and contribute to OCAD U's commitments to equity, inclusion, decolonization and the Scarborough Charter on Anti-Black Racism and Black Inclusion in Canadian Higher Education. The position also supports the development of partnerships and opportunities that enhance the student experience and strengthen connections between Black students, alumni, faculty, staff and community members. Summary of Responsibilities: Assist with the development and delivery of programs, events and initiatives that support Black student community-building and engagement. Support Black-identifying student groups and student leaders with event planning, promotion, logistics and administration. Coordinate meetings and provide administrative support to the Black student community-building steering committee, including agendas, minutes and action-item tracking. Develop promotional materials and communications that increase awareness of opportunities, resources and events for Black-identifying students. Support outreach efforts that encourage participation and strengthen connections among Black-identifying students, alumni, faculty, staff and community members. Contribute to data collection, feedback gathering and assessment activities that inform future programming and continuous improvement. Qualifications: University degree and/or equivalent combination of education, training and experience in a related field Preference will be given to recent graduates with demonstrated experience and lived understanding of Black community engagement and student leadership. Experience supporting programs, events, student engagement initiatives or community development activities. Excellent organizational, problem solving, interpersonal and customer service skills Excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Experience creating promotional, social media or communication materials. Proficiency with Microsoft 365 and standard office software applications. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment. Demonstrated commitment to equity, anti-racism, inclusion and student success. Knowledge of post-secondary student life programming, community engagement or peer leadership programs is considered an asset. Mode of Work: On Campus Compensation: Hiring Range - $20.02 to $23.67 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $20.02 to $27.17 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 14 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to begin September 30 th 2026 and continue until April 30th, 2027. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized administrators, priority in hiring will be given to qualified racialized persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code . OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3890": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Learning Skills Specialist Job Code 3890 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Student Writing, Communication and Learning Support, the Learning Skills Specialist develops, plans and delivers learning support programs for undergraduate and graduate students at OCAD University (OCAD U) to support their academic success and promote their engagement with scholarly, creative and disciplinary discourses and communities with an emphasis on reducing barriers to learning for disabled students, including those registered with Student Accessibility Services (SAS). The Learning Skills Specialist collaborates with program staff to build capacity for learning support across Centre programs and service areas and works closely with SAS staff to facilitate holistic student supports, contributing to initiatives that address the intersectional learning experiences and enhance access to learning for OCAD U’s diverse student body. This role broadly serves as a resource in the areas of student learning skills development and accessible student-centred pedagogy, informed by a commitment to decolonizing, anti-racist approaches and an understanding of Disability Studies theory and practice. The Learning Skills Specialist works within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) as part of a larger team delivering a range of programs and services for faculty, students, Teaching Assistants and other instructional staff. Together, they collaborate with the university community to provide leadership and support that creates and sustains equitable and engaging teaching and learning environments for all OCAD University students. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Summary of Responsibilities: Participate in portfolio-wide program planning processes with reference to emerging student needs, curricular changes and OCAD University’s Academic and Strategic Plan Develop and deliver learning support programs (e.g. one-on-one, small group, peer-based, co-curricular, classroom-based) to facilitate student learning skills development with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; consult with SAS staff and other stakeholders to identify ongoing and emerging student needs and to inform program development and delivery priorities Provide individual learning skills consultations for students drawing from learning strategy best practice Develop and provide student support, resources and learning opportunities in the use of learning technologies and applications for navigating and reducing barriers to learning and enhancing academic success Develop and deliver learning skills training and resources for program staff as part of annual pre-service and in-service tutor education to build capacity for learning skills support across program and service areas In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, develop and deliver student learning skills-building workshops as part of student transition, orientation and retention programming with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; collaborate with SAS to develop and deliver academic orientation and transition programming for students with disabilities Collaborate with the Lead, Co-curricular and Peer Learning Programs to develop and deliver academic skills workshops for students on probation Work closely with SAS to deliver student programming and faculty supports, as required, that strengthens access and accommodations processes for disabled students, including students registered with SAS Support the delivery of undergraduate academic integrity consultations and other resources as required; complete related reporting requirements Develop and maintain student learning resources to support their success in in person, blended and online learning environments and across all course types (e.g. studio, seminar, lecture) In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, contribute to the development and implementation of student learning support program policies and procedures Facilitate timely and effective student referrals within the Centre and between the Centre, SAS and other areas of the university Develop web content as required and contribute to promotional and outreach initiatives related to learning skills programs and services Undertake research to identify promising practices and provide expertise and guidance across the staff team and the University to support the integration of evidence-based practice; contribute to faculty and staff professional learning opportunities to promote best practices in student learning skills development and accessible  pedagogy, informed by equity-based and Disability Studies approaches to education, as required Contribute to the development and implementation of program monitoring and evaluation tools and participate in critically reflective practice and evidence-informed program development and decision-making Actively contribute to Learning Skills professional communities and conversations as well as best practice investigations and advancements in learning skills development and accessible pedagogy for art and design education; share research findings through a variety of knowledge dissemination activities Participate in reflective practice and professional learning activities, individually and in teams, to integrate equity and sustainability principles across relevant programs, processes and practices Fulfill Centre and Ministry reporting requirements Participate in departmental planning in support of the university’s strategic and academic plans and Centre goals and objectives; participate on relevant permanent or ad-hoc University committees, as required Assist the Manager and Director in other related tasks that contribute to the successful operation of the Centre and support the Centre’s strategic directions Qualifications: Master’s degree in Education, Social Sciences or equivalent; Supplemental Instruction, Learning Disability Specialist or Learning Strategist training certificate an asset A minimum of five (5) years of experience providing learning and academic skills building programs to diverse learners and students with disabilities in a post-secondary environment; progressive experience working in a university academic success centre or equivalent In-depth understanding of the learning and academic support needs of diverse learners and students with disabilities Post-secondary studies in Art or Design an asset; past or present art or design practice an asset Strong knowledge of best practices in student learning theory and practice; demonstrated experience developing and implementing co-curricular peer learning programming Informed understanding of the opportunities, limitations and risks associated with artificial intelligence (AI) for student learning and academic success; active engagement with evolving research, pedagogical practices and sector developments related to AI use in learning skills education and support. Demonstrated understanding of critical pedagogy, equity studies, accessible pedagogy and Universal Design for Learning (UDL); understanding of Disability Studies theory and practices with experience translating Disability Studies approaches into student program development and program delivery practices Excellent program development skills with experience conducting needs assessments and stakeholder engagement to inform multi-year program planning Demonstrated experience designing, delivering and evaluating skills-building workshops for students; excellent group facilitation and instructional skills Demonstrated capacity for and experience in providing assistance to students in app and learning technology use for learning and academic success; experience giving students support in the use of assistive technology, an asset Excellent communication, interpersonal and leadership skills, and a demonstrated ability to exercise tact, sound judgment and commitment to confidentiality Excellent academic and professional writing skills with the ability to write effectively for different audiences Demonstrated experience producing educational resources and handouts including experience developing learning resources to support online learning environments Knowledge of best practices in multilingual pedagogy and an understanding of the barriers and support needs of post-secondary students studying in a second language Track record of working consultatively with faculty to build relationships and support student program development and delivery; experience developing and delivering faculty and TA professional learning programming an asset Demonstrated experience developing and integrating decolonizing, anti-racist, accessible and translingual approaches and pedagogies in previous work or study Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, anti-racism, decolonization and accessibility; and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Demonstrated ability to take initiative, excellent organizational and time management skills Strong computer skills with knowledge of Microsoft 365 applications; knowledge of Canvas learning management system and Adobe Creative Cloud applications an asset Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: Hybrid. A minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.81 to $49.08 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits; Salary Range - $42.81 to $56.26 per hour. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until June 5 th , 2028. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3889": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Learning Skills Specialist Job Code 3889 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create. Please note that internal job postings are open only to current permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees and temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service. Reporting to the Manager, Student Writing, Communication and Learning Support, the Learning Skills Specialist develops, plans and delivers learning support programs for undergraduate and graduate students at OCAD University (OCAD U) to support their academic success and promote their engagement with scholarly, creative and disciplinary discourses and communities with an emphasis on reducing barriers to learning for disabled students, including those registered with Student Accessibility Services (SAS). The Learning Skills Specialist collaborates with program staff to build capacity for learning support across Centre programs and service areas and works closely with SAS staff to facilitate holistic student supports, contributing to initiatives that address the intersectional learning experiences and enhance access to learning for OCAD U’s diverse student body. This role broadly serves as a resource in the areas of student learning skills development and accessible student-centred pedagogy, informed by a commitment to decolonizing, anti-racist approaches and an understanding of Disability Studies theory and practice. The Learning Skills Specialist works within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) as part of a larger team delivering a range of programs and services for faculty, students, Teaching Assistants and other instructional staff. Together, they collaborate with the university community to provide leadership and support that creates and sustains equitable and engaging teaching and learning environments for all OCAD University students. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Summary of Responsibilities: Participate in portfolio-wide program planning processes with reference to emerging student needs, curricular changes and OCAD University’s Academic and Strategic Plan Develop and deliver learning support programs (e.g. one-on-one, small group, peer-based, co-curricular, classroom-based) to facilitate student learning skills development with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; consult with SAS staff and other stakeholders to identify ongoing and emerging student needs and to inform program development and delivery priorities Provide individual learning skills consultations for students drawing from learning strategy best practice Develop and provide student support, resources and learning opportunities in the use of learning technologies and applications for navigating and reducing barriers to learning and enhancing academic success Develop and deliver learning skills training and resources for program staff as part of annual pre-service and in-service tutor education to build capacity for learning skills support across program and service areas In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, develop and deliver student learning skills-building workshops as part of student transition, orientation and retention programming with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; collaborate with SAS to develop and deliver academic orientation and transition programming for students with disabilities Collaborate with the Lead, Co-curricular and Peer Learning Programs to develop and deliver academic skills workshops for students on probation Work closely with SAS to deliver student programming and faculty supports, as required, that strengthens access and accommodations processes for disabled students, including students registered with SAS Support the delivery of undergraduate academic integrity consultations and other resources as required; complete related reporting requirements Develop and maintain student learning resources to support their success in in person, blended and online learning environments and across all course types (e.g. studio, seminar, lecture) In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, contribute to the development and implementation of student learning support program policies and procedures Facilitate timely and effective student referrals within the Centre and between the Centre, SAS and other areas of the university Develop web content as required and contribute to promotional and outreach initiatives related to learning skills programs and services Undertake research to identify promising practices and provide expertise and guidance across the staff team and the University to support the integration of evidence-based practice; contribute to faculty and staff professional learning opportunities to promote best practices in student learning skills development and accessible  pedagogy, informed by equity-based and Disability Studies approaches to education, as required Contribute to the development and implementation of program monitoring and evaluation tools and participate in critically reflective practice and evidence-informed program development and decision-making Actively contribute to Learning Skills professional communities and conversations as well as best practice investigations and advancements in learning skills development and accessible pedagogy for art and design education; share research findings through a variety of knowledge dissemination activities Participate in reflective practice and professional learning activities, individually and in teams, to integrate equity and sustainability principles across relevant programs, processes and practices Fulfill Centre and Ministry reporting requirements Participate in departmental planning in support of the university’s strategic and academic plans and Centre goals and objectives; participate on relevant permanent or ad-hoc University committees, as required Assist the Manager and Director in other related tasks that contribute to the successful operation of the Centre and support the Centre’s strategic directions Qualifications: Master’s degree in Education, Social Sciences or equivalent; Supplemental Instruction, Learning Disability Specialist or Learning Strategist training certificate an asset A minimum of five (5) years of experience providing learning and academic skills building programs to diverse learners and students with disabilities in a post-secondary environment; progressive experience working in a university academic success centre or equivalent In-depth understanding of the learning and academic support needs of diverse learners and students with disabilities Post-secondary studies in Art or Design an asset; past or present art or design practice an asset Strong knowledge of best practices in student learning theory and practice; demonstrated experience developing and implementing co-curricular peer learning programming Informed understanding of the opportunities, limitations and risks associated with artificial intelligence (AI) for student learning and academic success; active engagement with evolving research, pedagogical practices and sector developments related to AI use in learning skills education and support. Demonstrated understanding of critical pedagogy, equity studies, accessible pedagogy and Universal Design for Learning (UDL); understanding of Disability Studies theory and practices with experience translating Disability Studies approaches into student program development and program delivery practices Excellent program development skills with experience conducting needs assessments and stakeholder engagement to inform multi-year program planning Demonstrated experience designing, delivering and evaluating skills-building workshops for students; excellent group facilitation and instructional skills Demonstrated capacity for and experience in providing assistance to students in app and learning technology use for learning and academic success; experience giving students support in the use of assistive technology, an asset Excellent communication, interpersonal and leadership skills, and a demonstrated ability to exercise tact, sound judgment and commitment to confidentiality Excellent academic and professional writing skills with the ability to write effectively for different audiences Demonstrated experience producing educational resources and handouts including experience developing learning resources to support online learning environments Knowledge of best practices in multilingual pedagogy and an understanding of the barriers and support needs of post-secondary students studying in a second language Track record of working consultatively with faculty to build relationships and support student program development and delivery; experience developing and delivering faculty and TA professional learning programming an asset Demonstrated experience developing and integrating decolonizing, anti-racist, accessible and translingual approaches and pedagogies in previous work or study Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, anti-racism, decolonization and accessibility; and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Demonstrated ability to take initiative, excellent organizational and time management skills Strong computer skills with knowledge of Microsoft 365 applications; knowledge of Canvas learning management system and Adobe Creative Cloud applications an asset Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location Compensation: OPSEU Unit 1, Appendix B, Grade B3; $42.81 to $56.26 per hour Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until June 5 th , 2028. Mode of Work: Hybrid. A minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Interested internal permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 or temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service applicants are invited to submit an updated *resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting “Apply Now” below, no later than Monday September 14 th , 2026 . * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3884": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Privacy Specialist Job Code 3884 Department Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Vice-President, Finance and Administration (VPFA), the Privacy Specialist acts as OCAD University’s subject matter expert on matters related to access to information, protection of privacy, and records management. Responsibilities include proper application of access and privacy legislation, processing access to information requests, promoting proper records retention and good record-keeping practices, completing privacy and security impact assessments, investigating privacy breaches and privacy complaints, and promoting a culture of privacy protection and good information governance at the University. The Privacy Specialist operates independently and exercises professional judgment while keeping the VPFA informed of significant privacy and records management matters, breaches, investigations, compliance issues and emerging institutional risks, and is responsible for ensuring the University is in compliance with relevant privacy and access legislation. The Specialist must have knowledge of legislative requirements and must maintain an ongoing awareness of developments in the areas of privacy and records management, and the implications of those developments for the University. Summary of Responsibilities : Privacy (70%) Independently provide authoritative advice on the University’s obligations under access and privacy legislation Ontario’s Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act (FIPPA) and Personal Health Information Protection Act (PHIPA) are collectively referred to as “Privacy Law”. Prepare regular reports, analyses and strategic advice for the VPFA regarding privacy compliance, records management, emerging risks, breaches, trends and information governance matters Develop institutional privacy frameworks and provide strategic advice and recommendations regarding privacy, information governance and related institutional risks Provide information, guidance and advice to the University community and general public regarding questions about privacy and information access requests at the University Respond to the University’s access for information requests, ensuring compliance with Privacy Law Provide guidance and information to requestors (both internal and external to the University) with their access requests Direct and coordinate the retrieval of information relevant to access requests with staff, faculty and administration Prepare responses to requests for information through review and consolidation of retrieved information Interpret Privacy Law in order to make informed decisions regarding the application of exemptions and exclusions to the information requested, if applicable, including researching relevant orders and judicial review decisions Respond and act on behalf of the University when decisions on access are appealed to the Information and Privacy Commissioner of Ontario (IPC) Receive and investigate privacy-related complaints, and respond to the IPC assigned analyst in cases of formal privacy complaints against the University Lead the response to privacy breaches at the University through the collection of relevant information and providing subject matter expertise to senior administrators for informed decision-making Work with IT in cases of cybersecurity incidents involving personal information. Coordinate any required involvement from legal counsel through liaising with the Office of the Vice-President, Finance and Administration Submit all mandatory reporting to the IPC and prepares annual reports on Privacy Law compliance for the Audit, Finance and Risk Committee of the Board of Governors Plan, lead and conduct Privacy Impact Assessments Work closely with Information Technology staff to evaluate and provide authoritative recommendations on Privacy and Security Impact Assessments for IT solutions Provide advice and recommendations on information technology projects, privacy implications of emerging technologies (i.e. AI solutions/applications) or other initiatives involving personal information being implemented across or in different units at the University, which may have an impact on privacy, confidentiality, or records retention Collaborate and communicate with privacy and access colleagues at other universities across Ontario to ensure best practices and up-to-date methods are maintained Keep up-to-date with new developments in Privacy Law, as well as directives from the IPC office Act as privacy/information subject matter expert on relevant committees and working groups Develop and provide training to member of the University community about their obligations under Privacy Law Records Management (30%) Develop and maintain the University’s records management framework by actively participating on the Records and Information Management Advisory Committee and overseeing existing policies and procedures as they relate to the RIM program Provides leadership and guidance to the University community on records management and retention best practices Advise on and recommend best practices for records management to departments, particularly in relation with Privacy Law Provide advice and assistance to units in the implementation of records management procedures and practices, including assisting with file plans, retention schedules, and the management of records Advise senior administration on records management issues as they affect long-term reliability and accessibility of University records Act as subject matter expert on record management on relevant committees and working groups Promote and encourage awareness of the role and importance of records management in the University community through training, education and effective communication Monitor and audit the records management program at the University Stay up-to-date on best practices in the field of records management and information governance Continually monitor changes and new developments in provincial and federal statutes and regulations that affect the management and retention of University records Qualifications : Undergraduate degree required; a graduate degree and/or certification in a related field (e.g. CIPP/C, CRM, MLIS) is considered an asset Minimum of six (6) years of experience in the field of privacy and records management, preferably in a public sector environment Experience reviewing and interpreting relevant statutes, regulations and case law Extensive knowledge of FIPPA and PHIPA, with some knowledge of Canada’s Anti-Spam Legislation, General Data Protection Regulation, Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act and related legislation and regulations Demonstrated research and analytical skills Demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive and confidential information in an appropriate and effective manner Ability to determine the dissemination of various types of sensitive and/or confidential information Strong communication skills, including the ability to explain complex matters in plain language and the ability to write clearly and succinctly in a variety of formats (policies, procedures, bulletins, training guides, etc.) Expected to be self-directed in responding to requests, complaints, breaches, and questions Exceptional organizational skills and the ability to work independently while coordinating one’s own work with that of several offices Professional demeanor and ability to relate effectively with senior administrators, staff, faculty and external stakeholders Thorough knowledge of records management theory and methodology Ability to prioritize activities, recognize urgent matters amongst multiple requests, all with competing deadlines and prioritizes information that needs to be dealt with immediately Familiarity with information technology as it relates to matters of privacy, security and protection of personal information Strong project management and change management skills Strong focus on excellence in customer service and project leadership Understanding of the business requirements of an academic institution and the ability to identify the needs of university departments Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting an inclusive work and learning environment Must be willing and able to provide regular flexibility with respect to hours of work Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: Hiring Range - $84,311.75 to $96,656.35 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $84,311.75 to $110,808.36 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below, no later than Monday September 21 st , 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3881": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Program Assistant, FOD Job Code 3881 Department Faculty of Design Office Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Faculty of Design Office, the Program Assistant is responsible for acting as the initial point of contact for students, across all program areas within the Faculty of Design. Summary of Responsibilities: Act as the initial point of contact for all Design programs by responding to student inquiries and requests for information, directing inquiries as appropriate Responsible for sending communication to students (i.e. academic instructions, dates and deadlines) Provide direct support for students, including scheduling appointments with Chairs, supporting Reinstatements, maintaining related files, accessing student files, transcripts and registration information as required Assist the Manager with Awards Jurying Working collaboratively with other Faculty of Design Office staff, to support improvements to office procedures to increase efficiency, and workflow balance Prepare and maintain accurate digital filing systems Liaise with various departments including the Office of the Registrar, Student Accessibility Services, Student Wellness Centre and International Student Support & Exchange Programs to address student concerns Assist with FOD’s participation in the Graduate Exhibition, under the direction of the Manager Support the review and processing of student requests for program changes, reinstatement, and entry into Faculty of Design programs under the Manager’s oversight; prepare and organize required documentation, ensuring all materials are reviewed by the Manager prior to submission to the Office of the Registrar Provide administrative support to the Associate Dean, Academic Affairs including support related to academic misconduct, grade appeals, student petitions and elevated faculty/student issues Act as the initial contact for individuals and groups bringing issues to the Office of the Dean, and advise regarding the most appropriate avenues within the University’s administrative and academic structures to handle them efficiently Serve as secretary to faculty office, program and University committees, as directed by the Manager, Faculty of Design Office Generate reports and retrieve curricular and scheduling information using University systems (e.g. Canvas Cloud, Colleague UI, Self‑Service/Student Planning, CROA, and room‑booking platforms) Perform general administrative duties such as: photocopying; booking rooms; preparing and following up on work requests; processing incoming and outgoing mail; ordering office and photocopier supplies; etc. Act as back-up to the Program Assistant (Admin) as required Support the Manager in daily operations, special projects and other duties that contribute to the successful operation of the Faculty of Design Office Qualifications: Post-secondary degree or equivalent, with two (2) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service in a diverse environment Experience using learning management systems to support faculty and students; experience with Canvas, an asset Strong computer skills with a knowledge of Microsoft Office applications (Outlook, Word, Excel), and experience working with databases Demonstrated project management skills pertaining to organizing international collaborations, special events, competitions etc. Outstanding interpersonal, communication, and problem-solving skills Demonstrated attention to detail, and organizational skills, including the ability to juggle competing demands, prioritize tasks and meet deadlines Proven ability to take initiative and work both independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promotion a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty This posting is for an existing vacancy. Compensation: Hiring Range - $27.56 to $31.53 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $27.56 to $35.76 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to begin September 2026 and continue until June 30 th , 2027. Mode of Work: Hybrid, 3 days on campus. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3891": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Student Advisor Job Code 3891 Department Office of the Registrar Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create Please note that internal job postings are open only to current permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees and temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service. Reporting to the Associate Registrar, Student Advising & Special Projects, and working closely with Faculty Offices and Student Affairs staff across the University, the Student Advisor supports student success and persistence through guidance on course selection, program requirements, degree planning, and academic policy. Through one-to-one advising, group sessions, and the development of information resources and tools, the Student Advisor helps undergraduate students navigate institutional processes and connects them with the resources they need to pursue their academic, creative, and personal goals toward program completion. Student Advising is committed to decolonization and to centring the student journey. The Student Advisor plays a key role in ongoing efforts to dismantle systemic and structural barriers and to foster diversity, equity, and inclusion in how the University engages and supports students. Summary of Responsibilities : Deliver comprehensive advising on course selection, academic standing, program and degree requirements, registration, and available supports, asking probing questions and presenting a range of options and their possible consequences Advise students experiencing academic challenges, including steps to regain good standing and referrals to appropriate resources, taking into account personal circumstances and individual background Help students explore options and build personalized action plans aligned with their academic and creative goals Advise on the appropriateness, precedents, and process for petitions and appeals, and suggest alternative courses of action where appropriate Develop content for and facilitate workshops and information sessions Serve as a liaison between Faculty Offices and students to support an integrated model of advising Maintain regular communication with other student service areas and with records and registration staff to foster close working relationships and ensure timely, appropriate referrals Maintain accurate student records, including advising notes, in compliance with University regulations on privacy and confidentiality Maintain current knowledge of University policies, procedures, and best practices in student advising Support the Associate Registrar with other tasks contributing to the successful operation of the advising function Qualifications: Post-secondary degree in a related field, with a minimum of three (3) years' related experience in a post-secondary environment Direct experience and deep awareness of the structural and systemic barriers faced by marginalized communities Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, accessibility, and inclusive practice Experience advising current students on academic matters, with a demonstrated ability to problem-solve Experience with student information systems (e.g., Colleague Self-Service) and familiarity with registrarial policies and procedures at the post-secondary level Experience preparing and leading presentations, seminars, and/or workshops Proficiency in the Office 365 suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, OneDrive) and SharePoint Strong oral, written, and visual communication skills Strong interpersonal and analytical skills Demonstrated professionalism, with the ability to exercise tact, good judgement, and diplomacy Strong organizational and time management skills, with attention to detail and the ability to meet deadlines under pressure of high volume and shifting priorities Demonstrated ability to take initiative and contribute actively to the planning and implementation of student-centred services Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively within a team environment Compensation: OPSEU Unit I, Appendix A, Grade A4; $57,486.45 to $75,566.13 per annum Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. Mode of Work: Hybrid Interested internal permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 or temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service applicants are invited to submit an updated *resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting “Apply Now” below, no later than Thursday September 17, 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3888": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Teaching Assistant (Tutorial Leader): 2D Game Design, Faculty of Arts & Science, Fall 2026 Job Code 3888 Department Faculty of Arts & Science Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Arts & Science invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Fall 2026 semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and meeting target registration in courses. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. DIGF-2013-301: 2D Game Design (Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – January 4, 2027; Wednesdays: 3:10PM - 6:10PM; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: Games are a major entertainment form of the 21st century. As a design discipline, digital game form combines game worlds, rule sets and play to actively engage a player in an ongoing gameplay experience. Students will iteratively design, visualize, develop, document and test unique game concepts to a final proof of concept stage. Developing skills from paper prototyping, game modelling and level design through to storyboarding, asset creation, character design and animation, this course provides a solid foundation in game design methodology for use both within and beyond the game industry. Students with credit in GDES-3063 or GDES-3B70 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Meet regularly with the faculty and students on grading, course content and other matters as needed; assist in the evaluation of student work, using criteria established by the supervising faculty; work with faculty and other academic staff in support of the University’s educational goals and objectives; participate in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. QUALIFICATIONS: The ideal candidate should have a graduate degree, undergraduate degree or be currently enrolled in a relevant graduate program; a demonstrated understanding of pedagogy, e.g. course preparation, teaching methods, evaluations standards, etc., English as a Second Language training is considered an asset; strong organizational, interpersonal, and oral and written communication skills; sound knowledge of, and commitment to, healthy and safe work practices; previous teaching assistance experience at the post-secondary level. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Teaching Assistants are hired annually on a per-course basis with fixed-term annual contracts renewable to a maximum of 3 years. Please note: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). One cannot hold both a Teaching Assistant and Faculty Appointment within the same semester. All positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. HOURS OF WORK: Hours per week are based on enrollment. COMPENSATION: $ 33.78 per hour, 6% in lieu of benefits, and vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying specific relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae The names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
